From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 12:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] AMLCodec: write a packet's header and pts once, report the
 outcome

newflag is never cleared (clearing it stops the pts check-in for every
packet after the first), so each retry of a packet after a full stream
buffer checked its pts in again and wrote its header (WMV3, VC-1,
DivX 3, MJPEG, MPEG-4 in Matroska) again, in the middle of the unit. The
packet now notes what of it is in the decoder: nothing, its pts, or some
of its bytes. The pts is checked in only while nothing is in, the header
written only while none of its bytes are, and the note lasts until the
packet is added or dropped or the decoder reset, so a packet fed again
after a stall goes on from where it stopped rather than repeating
either. The stream header written when the decoder opens or resets
belongs to no packet and leaves the note clear.

A packet told to retry is now fed again as it was handed to the decoder
the first time. The wrapper keeps a copy of that buffer, after the
bitstream conversion and the MVC frame_num rewrite, and when the player
hands back the same packet it sends the copy straight to the decoder,
without running the conversion, the metadata latches, the subtitle depth
or the HDR10+ check-in on it again. Run a second time, the converter
leaves out the parameter sets it puts in front of the first IDR picture,
and the rewriter and the dual-layer conversion carry state from one
packet to the next. The decoder is told whether a packet is fed again.
The copy is freed when the codec is closed or reset.

A packet that finds the stream buffer full, the common refusal in steady
playback, is now turned away before anything runs on it, so it is
neither converted nor copied; its size there includes a dual-layer
enhancement layer. The buffer level latch and the stream mode prebuffer
threshold are now set by that check, for every packet offered. AddData
runs it again on the packet as converted, so it still refuses one that
the conversion made too big for what is left, and the first packet,
which comes before the decoder opens, is checked there. A failed read of
the buffer state, which reads as full, no longer sets them.

A conversion that left nothing to decode was handed on as an empty
packet, refused, and retried for good. It is now dropped.

A write that failed outright after part of the access unit went in broke
the write loop and AddData returned true: the truncated unit stayed in
the stream buffer, the rest of it was dropped and the next unit was
parsed straight after it. A failure after the pts was checked in left a
pts without data the same way, as did a packet fed again whose header
could then not be prepared. Such a packet now resets the decoder; one
that put nothing in is dropped, as before.

A stall, the decoder taking nothing for 100 attempts, now leaves the
packet to be fed again as long as none of its data went in, whether or
not its pts or header did, rather than resetting the decoder. A header
write that gives up the same way with nothing written is the same stall;
it used to drop the packet. A stall after part of the data went in still
resets the decoder, and a packet completed on the last of the 100
attempts no longer does. A stall that goes on is ended by the decoder
timeout in GetPicture, which the player calls between the attempts; the
attempts themselves do not count as a break in its calls.

AddData now says what became of the packet: added, dropped for good, to
be fed again, or to be fed again after a reset, which the wrapper now
does. The wrapper switches on it, so a new outcome cannot go unhandled.
A dropped packet is no longer counted by the seek preroll nor given
frame metadata. A packet that needs a reset again when it is fed to the
reset decoder is dropped rather than fed again for good. Only that same
packet counts: once a packet is added, dropped, or set aside before it
reaches the decoder, the next reset is a first one again.

The extradata a VVC stream sends with its first packet is now freed only
once a packet took it into the decoder: a first packet dropped before
anything went in lost it.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>

